    "title": "Chicago Bears Seek Public Funding for New Stadium",
    "text": "• The Chicago Bears are seeking $855 million in public funding for infrastructure.\n• The funding is intended for building an enclosed stadium in Arlington Heights.\n• The stadium could host Super Bowls and Final Fours.\n• The Bears plan to pay for the stadium itself but need assistance for project completion.\n• The report was released by real estate consulting firm HR&A Advisors Inc.\n• The $855 million would cover costs for roads, sewers, and changes to a commuter rail line.\n• Team president Kevin Warren stated that 2023 is the year to finalize plans for the stadium.\n• Warren mentioned the team could bid to host a Super Bowl as soon as 2031.\n• The stadium construction would require zero state money, according to Warren.\n• A bill needs to be passed in October to freeze property taxes for large-scale construction projects.\n• Warren claims the bill could create 56,000 jobs during construction and 9,000 permanent jobs.\n• The Bears' focus for a new home has shifted between Arlington Heights and Soldier Field.\n• In September 2022, the Bears unveiled a nearly $5 billion plan for Arlington Heights.\n• The team had previously considered a new stadium next to Soldier Field but returned focus to Arlington Heights.\n• The Bears have never owned their stadium since moving to Chicago in 1921.",
